{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Tobias lifted the photograph toward the window, tilting it until the light caught the woman's face. \"That's the pier at Harrow Bay,\" he said. \"Look at the lighthouse behind her. The striped one.\" Mira leaned closer. The girl in the picture couldn't have been more than nineteen, her hair pinned loosely and her coat buttoned wrong, as if she'd dressed in a hurry. \"I never knew she lived by the sea,\" Mira murmured. \"She told us she'd never seen the ocean until she was forty.\" \"Maybe she lied.\" Tobias set the photograph down gently, as though it might crack. \"Or maybe she just didn't want us asking.\" Mira picked up the compass. It was heavier than she expected, its brass case dented along one edge, the glass faintly scratched. When she opened it, the needle trembled, then swung, not north, but toward the second box beneath the eaves. She frowned and turned in a slow circle. The needle held steady, pointing stubbornly at the stack. \"Tobias,\" she said quietly. \"Is it broken?\" He took it from her, turned it over, and shook it once. The needle returned to the same spot. For a long moment neither of them spoke. Rain began to tap against the round window, soft at first, then steadier. Tobias untied the letters and slid the top envelope free. It was addressed to their grandmother, postmarked Harrow Bay, dated fifty-two years ago. On the back, in faded ink, someone had written a single line: Don't open the third box alone."}
